result.components:
I adopted no component from candidates.json, and I added no adaptation from the list. Each one is either already part of Scuttlebutt or does not fit it:
- digest-repair and pull-sweep-join: the design is already per-origin digest reconciliation. A joiner's empty digest is answered from version 0, which is a full pull, and joiners already reach parity in about 0.5 s.
- bp-skip: a cell is sent only when a digest shows the peer lacks it, so it never goes back to its origin or to a peer that holds it.
- error-budget-emit: this is the shared scheduler, which the arm keeps (per-key in the final commit).
- departed-pruning: discovery removes peers, and an origin's dictionary entry and watermark are freed when its last cell expires.
- retransmit-limit: a push-gossip concept with no meaning in pull anti-entropy.

The one change taken from a cited source is the request reply (173974e). It is Cassandra's GossipDigestAck, one of the arm's own references, and is documented with its reason in the README design notes. It keeps every invariant:
- cells move only in answer to a digest;
- per-origin delivery stays in version order (prefix property);
- digests stay per origin;
- exchanges stay pairwise;
- the SYN digest is still sent every round even when nothing differs.

Only the redundant second full digest is dropped. The watermark sweep and the stored-claim relay (bc5cc83) and the u32::MAX claim (15dd4de) refine the branch's existing UDP adaptation, which exists so a digest never claims a version its sender lacks. All three are documented in the 'Adaptations to UDP' section of the README.
